\title{Alpha scope: how the fade gradient is normalised across rows}
\description{
Several fade geoms accept an \code{alpha_scope} argument that controls how the
alpha gradient is \emph{normalised} — that is, which subset of the rendered
shapes the most-opaque end of the gradient is calibrated to. The vocabulary
overlaps but is not identical across the geom families, because what counts
as a meaningful "reference" differs between bars, areas, and ridgelines.
This page is the consolidated reference.
\subsection{What \code{alpha_scope} does}{

Each fade geom interpolates the rendered alpha between two extremes:
\code{alpha_fade_to} (the faded end, default fully transparent) and the row's
aesthetic \code{alpha} value (the opaque end, default fully opaque). The peak of
that interpolation is positioned somewhere on the data, and \code{alpha_scope}
chooses where:
\itemize{
\item Per-row scopes anchor the peak at the row's own extreme (each shape
gets the full alpha range to itself).
\item Group-relative scopes anchor the peak at the maximum within a discrete
subset (rows in the same subset share an alpha range; smaller rows
appear proportionally fainter).
\item Layer-wide scopes anchor the peak at the maximum across the whole layer
(or all panels under faceting).
}
}

\subsection{Vocabulary by geom family}{

The accepted values and defaults are family-specific:

\describe{
\item{\strong{Area family} (\code{\link[=geom_area_fade]{geom_area_fade()}}, \code{\link[=geom_density_fade]{geom_density_fade()}},
\code{\link[=geom_freqpoly_fade]{geom_freqpoly_fade()}})}{
Default \code{"global"}. Allowed: \code{"global"}, \code{"group"}.
\code{"global"} scales every group to the layer-wide maximum
\verb{|y|}, so equal \verb{|y|} always maps to equal alpha.
\code{"group"} lets each group use the full alpha range independently.
}
\item{\strong{Bar family} (\code{\link[=geom_col_fade]{geom_col_fade()}}, \code{\link[=geom_bar_fade]{geom_bar_fade()}})}{
Default \code{"bar"}. Allowed: \code{"bar"}, \code{"group"}, \code{"x"}, \code{"y"},
\code{"fill"}, \code{"colour"}, \code{"global"}.
\code{"bar"} gives every bar its own range (every peak hits full opacity);
\code{"x"} / \code{"y"} normalise within a position-axis cluster (useful for
stacks and dodges); \code{"fill"} / \code{"colour"} normalise within a colour
class; \code{"global"} normalises layer-wide; \code{"group"} falls back to
\code{ggplot2}'s \code{data$group}.
}
\item{\strong{Histogram family} (\code{\link[=geom_histogram_fade]{geom_histogram_fade()}})}{
Default \code{"bar"}. Allowed: \code{"bar"}, \code{"group"}, \code{"bin"}, \code{"fill"},
\code{"colour"}, \code{"global"}. The shared bar-family scopes carry over,
but \code{"x"} / \code{"y"} are \strong{not} accepted: they key on \code{round(data$x|y)}
which is meaningless on a continuous binned axis. Use \code{"bin"} instead
— it normalises within each bin (every cluster of dodged bars in one
bin shares an alpha range), recovering the per-cluster intent that
\code{"x"} / \code{"y"} give on \code{geom_col_fade()} / \code{geom_bar_fade()}.
}
\item{\strong{Ridgeline family} (\code{\link[=geom_ridgeline_fade]{geom_ridgeline_fade()}},
\code{\link[=geom_ridgeline_density_fade]{geom_ridgeline_density_fade()}})}{
Default \code{"group"}. Allowed: \code{"group"}, \code{"global"}.
\code{"group"} lets each ridge use the full alpha range
independently. \code{"global"} scales relative to the tallest ridge in the
entire layer (incl. across facets), so shorter ridges fade in
proportion.
}
}
