\subsection{Why the same name means different things}{

\code{"global"} always means \emph{the layer-wide maximum} — but the \emph{maximum of
what} depends on the geom: \verb{|y|} for areas, \verb{|y|} (or \verb{|x|} under
\code{orientation = "y"}) for bars and histograms, and the tallest ridge
\code{height} for ridgelines. \code{"group"} always means \emph{normalise per \code{ggplot2}
group} (\code{data$group}, the interaction of all discrete aesthetics). For
ridgelines this is effectively "per ridge" because each \code{data$group}
corresponds to one ridge. The defaults above are chosen so that the most
common usage of each family produces sensible output without explicit
\code{alpha_scope}.
}
